-- Version 25 → 26: the system administrator becomes the owner of every project.
--
-- LNL-107 introduces `project_owner`, the per-project role senior to
-- `project_admin` — it holds a project's identity (name, prefix, visibility), its
-- repository, its deletion, and the granting of the two senior roles. The ticket
-- asks that on migration the sys admin become the owner of all projects, so that
-- every board has an owner of record from the moment the role exists, rather than
-- an owner-shaped hole that only a sys admin's blanket rights happen to fill.
--
-- ── Why this migration exists at all, when 11.sqm's did not ─────────────────
--
-- Roles are seeded, not migrated: `roles` is filled unconditionally at startup by
-- RoleStore.seed() with INSERT OR IGNORE, so a new Role enum value arrives on a
-- fresh volume and a deployed one by the same path with nothing to keep in step.
-- 11.sqm says exactly this, and `project_admin` needed no migration for that
-- reason. `project_owner` is different in one way only: it must be *granted* to
-- existing sys admins, and a grant is a row in `project_roles` that names a
-- `role_id` — which cannot exist until the role row does. Seeding runs *after*
-- migrations, so at this point the role is not there yet.
--
-- So this migration seeds the role itself first, and the description string below
-- MUST match the Role.PROJECT_OWNER enum's verbatim: seed() is INSERT OR IGNORE
-- keyed on role_key, so whichever of the two writes first wins and the other is a
-- no-op. If they disagreed, a migrated instance would carry a different sentence
-- under the checkbox than a fresh one, and nothing would ever reconcile them. The
-- INSERT OR IGNORE here means seed()'s later call is the harmless no-op, not this.
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO roles (role_key, description)
VALUES ('project_owner', 'Own this project: everything an administrator can do, plus its name, its repository, and deleting it.');

-- Grant it to every system administrator on every project. A cross join, because
-- "the sys admin owns all projects" is literally every (sys admin, project) pair.
-- INSERT OR IGNORE so a re-run — or a deployment with a single sys admin who is
-- already, somehow, a member — cannot fail on the composite primary key.
--
-- Read off `users.is_sys_admin`, the flag 11.sqm renamed. An instance with no sys
-- admin, or no projects, inserts nothing, which is correct: there is no owner to
-- seat and nothing to seat them on. This runs once; an owner deliberately revoked
-- later stays revoked, because a migration is not a startup seed.
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO project_roles (user_id, project_id, role_id)
SELECT users.id, projects.id, roles.id
FROM users
CROSS JOIN projects
JOIN roles ON roles.role_key = 'project_owner'
WHERE users.is_sys_admin = 1;
